WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
I don't think I ever have a particular  set of points as to a character that I  want to play there's no hard and fast  rules it's will have to be something  that hopefully sparks my interest that I  feel is outside of the kind of gender  norms that we've seen portrayed in our  entertainment as something that teaches  us about what it is to be human if I can  see a spark of authentic humanity in the  character then that makes me really want  to play it  I find the audition process incredibly  difficult and intimidating and it wasn't  always something I had a huge amount of  practice that either I think as time has  gone by really just investing in the  work and finding something that sparks  your interest I remember that's what my  art teacher taught me was make it  personal and then if you've got that to  hang on to then you can stop worrying  about how you can't stop blushing and  you want to go to the toilet all the  time a friend told me in 2011 that there  was something written about me on the  Internet  being in an HBO show and so I looked it  up I'd never done anything like that  before  and I saw all of these incredible fan  forums featuring my name I googled the  character I thought you should just read  the books first that is my training but  I couldn't wait I had to know what the  character was  and that really blew my mind the idea  that there might be this portrayal of a  woman in mainstream entertainment he was  entirely unconventional who was  physically very strong there wasn't the  most interesting thing about them was  the interesting thing about her is that  that is coupled with a extreme  sensitivity vulnerability and while she  was achieving these huge and impressive  and deeply skilled physical feats that  she was slowly developing as a person as  a woman and as a human like a sort of  rite of passage that she goes through  and also it  examine what it is to be unconventional  particularly as a woman within society  what interested me the most which George  on our Martin did so brilliantly and  David and Dan have done wonderfully is  that it was about overcoming the  obstacle it wasn't just about being  mocked for being outside of society it  was about overcoming that obstacle and  changing attitudes and that made me want  to play the part so badly I then read  all of Brian's story I read as much of  the books I could I think I read up to  the end of book three  I read voraciously I started physically  training this is all before the audition  how could I do anything all of them that  take that amazing opportunity seriously  the second audition actually lasted  maybe five minutes and I thought okay so  I've got that or that just went so so  badly so so badly I found out I got the  job and then the real work began and I  started training with the brilliant Cece  Smith who's a coaching directing  stuntman legend and he taught me how to  fight how to sword fight we worked on  the way I walked the way I held my body  and he really pushed me so I was able to  achieve things I never thought I could  like fighting a man that six foot seven  and weighs 20 T stone sorry Rory  preparation involved many many different  things it can involve as it has done  most prominently on Game of Thrones a  lot of physical training so I have done  a lot of physical training and reside  quite firmly in the physical world which  is not somewhere that I did before and  that's become a part of my life however  I did go to Drama Centre London which is  a classical training with a method  approach and we did lot of physical  training there and that's where I became  first aware that the body is a tool to  be used in its entirety so the greater  control you have over your body the more  precise your physical portrayal can be  and that isn't just about  playing someone strong or someone that  can do fighting or anything like that  it's really about maintaining control  over a different physical attitude when  I first started on Game of Thrones it  wasn't a small production but it wasn't  the vast machine that it is now but in  those days where the show wasn't in  enormous global success for instance you  noted a working season 2 season 3 and it  really felt they could a group of actors  thrown together all working hard to  craft the scenes to get the most out of  the scenes talked to really work this  drama and make that come to life the key  to successful collaboration with your  other actors on set I don't know I mean  I think it's about being generous part  of my training was about the ensemble  was about being perfect and ensemble and  about it not just being about you but if  you give the emphasis to other people if  you give the power to other people in  the scene who are all doing that's when  it starts to sing and having a good  laugh always helps the writing has been  absolutely incredible and I feel really  privileged to have played that part and  to have received that kind of writing to  work with the storyline with Jamie  Lannister has been incredible and the  scenes I've left love best have been my  scenes with Nikolaj coster-waldau  because he's taught me so much about  acting I know you're gonna love me  saying that and that he has may mean how  not to do it no I'm joking he has taught  me so much and it's been amazing to have  that sort of play going on but to work  with an actor who's so committed and so  generous and so caring towards you and  wants to make sure that you are that you  are safe and also to make you not safe  and I think the bathtub scene was an  amazing scene actually getting to watch  Nikolai's work at such close quarters  and to leave myself open and really be  deeply affected by that performance also  fighting the bear also fighting the  hound  it's very difficult for me to talk about  the most challenging aspects of being on  the show because I am hugely lucky to  have done such a thing and and I truly I  truly am grateful and it has changed my  life you know that kind of overrides  everything else because you're aware of  how privileged you are to be in that  position it can be difficult it can be  tough you know you're outside a lot I  don't really mind that but um obviously  you know there's a there's a lot of  costume to wear and obviously a lot of  the fights can feel quite real shooting  those can be incredibly painful but look  at what it makes so no complaints any  kind of advice that I might give and I  certainly don't really feel like I'm the  right person to do this but one thing I  have learnt is never never never never  give up and never change the person that  you are obviously you know evolve or die  but be your authentic self and sometimes  the world does change and sometimes  attitudes do change I hope we see  greater diversity on our screens and I  hope we see more developed characters  for those other than the usual sorts of  people we're used to seeing  [Music]

